 ## 5. Mutation results
 
-33 plausible defects were injected one at a time into the throwaway reference implementation (per-mutant failing
-tests in `validation/mutation-results.txt`).
-
-**28 killed.** Among them:
-- Gauss weights or nodes wrong; initial partition of 4 panels;
-- tolerance ignored or min instead of max; no refinement; budget off by one split; `evaluations()` inexact; always
-  converged; coarse panel value accepted;
-- unscaled norm;
-- no inversion iterations, or only 2;
-- seam repeated; uniform parameters rounded differently; last parameter not forced;
-- closed loop with 0 samples; `maxSamples` not enforced; internal array exposed; translation dropped; no zero-length
-  fallback; `parameterAt(length)` inexact; smallest error refined first;
-- validation bounds missing; no saturation;
-- no monotone clamp, and the Newton step sign wrong.
-
-The first run left survivors, and three tests were added for them:
-- `EqualDistanceTest.monotoneUnderLooseTolerance`, for M16;
-- two uniform ranges with `t0 + (t1 - t0) != t1`, for M18;
-- `M4AdversarialTest.newtonEfficiency`, for M28.
-
-**4 equivalent mutants**, which cannot change any result:
-- the non-finite check (the length becomes non-finite anyway);
-- the strict panel search;
-- `Math.round` (Java's rounding is round half up);
-- the `lengthAt(t0)` special case.
-
-**1 tolerance-equivalent mutant (M13):** the partial integral is not normalised by `I2/I1`. It violates the normative
-order of contract 5.5 but changes results only inside the panel error estimate. No independent test can see it
-without copying the algorithm, so it is an item for the implementation review.
+**Revision 2.** 47 plausible defects were injected one at a time into the throwaway R2 reference implementation:
+- the 33 R1 mutants, adapted to the R2 code;
+- 14 new R2 mutants: no tolerance floor; ratio mode as in R1, or one-sided; no clamp to `I2`; `r` not clamped; the
+  last iterate returned; ties to `lo`; Newton in every iteration; pure bisection; no tolerance exit; `lo` always
+  returned; no break at the cap; tolerance `tau` instead of `tau 2^-10`; no end acceptance.
+
+Per-mutant failing tests are in `validation/mutation-results.txt`.
+
+**42 killed.** Among them:
+- every R1 kill;
+- every R2 rule: M34 floor, M35 and M36 ratio mode, M37 clamp, M39 and M44 return rule, M40 ties, M41 and M42
+  phases, M43 and M46 tolerance, M47 end acceptance.
+
+**Survivors that turned out to change behaviour got tests** (all in `M4R2RegressionTest`):
+
+| Mutant | Test |
+|---|---|
+| M26 | `stationaryTail` |
+| M36 | new cases in `pathologicalPanels` |
+| M37 | `partialClampedToPanel` |
+| M40 | `tieGoesToUpperEnd` |
+| M12 | `nonFiniteAfterBuild` |
+| M15 | the middle stationary interval in `stationaryTail` |
+
+**Correction of R1.** R1 had classified M12 (the non-finite check) and M15 (the strict panel search) as equivalent.
+That was wrong:
+- M12 matters for `lengthAt` and `parameterAt` after a successful build;
+- M15 matters for a distance at the start of a stationary interval.
+
+**4 equivalent mutants**, which cannot change any result (reasons in `mutation-results.txt`):
+- M20, `Math.round`;
+- M33, the `lengthAt(t0)` special case;
+- M38, the clamp of `r`, which the end acceptance makes redundant;
+- M45, the break at the cap.
+
+**1 tolerance-equivalent mutant (M13):** the partial integral is not scaled by `rho`. It violates the normative order
+of contract 5.5, but changes results only inside the panel error estimate. No independent test can see it without
+copying the algorithm, so it is an item for the implementation review.
+
+**Revision 1** (for reference): 33 mutants, with 28 killed, 4 classified as equivalent and 1 tolerance-equivalent.
